["Here is a basic implementation of your request in C# using XmlReader and Dictionary:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Xml;
using System.Linq;

public static class XmlParser
{
    public static Dictionary<string, List<XPathNavigator>> Parse(Stream xmlData, string elementName, string attributeName)
    {
        var result = new Dictionary<string, List<XPathNavigator>>();

        using (XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(xmlData))
        {
            while (reader.Read())
            {
                if (reader.NodeType == XmlNodeType.Element && reader.Name == elementName)
                {
                    string attributeValue = reader.GetAttribute(attributeName);

                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(attributeValue))
                        continue;

                    List<XPathNavigator> elements;

                    if (!result.TryGetValue(attributeValue, out elements))
                    {
                        elements = new List<XPathNavigator>();
                        result[attributeValue] = elements;
                    }

                    var navigator = reader.ReadSubtree().Clone();

                    // Move to the first node in subtree, which should be the original element
                    navigator.MoveToContent(); 

                    if (!elements.Any(n => n.OuterXml == navigator.OuterXml))
                        elements.Add(navigator);
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```
This code reads the XML document one node at a time using XmlReader and only loads the relevant parts of the subtree into memory when adding to the collection. This allows it to handle large documents without loading the entire thing into memory, but note that this also means it can't be used with an XPathDocument or other document-based XML APIs where you might have access to full nodes/elements in isolation.

For logging, you would need to implement additional logic using a logging framework like NLog or log4net. This is outside the scope of this code as it would significantly complicate it and depends on the requirements for what to actually log.
"]

['Here's how you could write the code for this task in Java using the Fork/Join Framework (available from Java 7 onwards):

```java
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter;

public class MD5Hasher {
    private static final ExecutorService executor = ForkJoinPool.commonPool();

    public static Map<String, String> computeHashes(List<String> strings) {
        if (strings == null || strings.isEmpty()) {
            return Collections.emptyMap();
        }

        ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> results = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(strings.size());

        for (final String string : strings) {
            executor.execute(() -> {
                try {
                    MessageDigest md5 = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
                    byte[] digest = md5.digest(string.getBytes());
                    results.put(string, DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(digest));
                } finally {
                    latch.countDown();
                }
            });
        }

        try {
            latch.await();  // wait until all tasks are completed
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        return results;
    }
}
```
This code uses a ForkJoinPool common to the application, which is already designed for efficient concurrency and works well in multi-threaded environments. It also uses `ConcurrentHashMap` instead of regular HashMaps to guarantee thread safety without the need for synchronization. The CountDownLatch ensures that we only return after all tasks have completed.
']

