["Here's a JavaScript function that implements the above requirements:

```javascript
function copyBytes(chunks, startIndex, length) {
  let totalLength = chunks.reduce((accum, chunk) => accum + Buffer.byteLength(chunk), 0);
  if (startIndex < 0 || startIndex >= totalLength || startIndex + length > totalLength) throw new Error('Out of bounds');

  const resultBuffer = [];
  let bytesLeft = length;
  for (let i = 0, offset = startIndex; bytesLeft > 0 && i < chunks.length;) {
    if (!chunks[i]) continue; // Skip over null or undefined chunks

    const chunkStartOffset = Math.max(0, offset - Buffer.byteLength(chunks[i]));
    const bytesToCopy = Math.min(bytesLeft, Buffer.byteLength(chunks[i]) - chunkStartOffset);

    resultBuffer.push(Buffer.from(chunks[i].slice(chunkStartOffset, chunkStartOffset + bytesToCopy)));
    bytesLeft -= bytesToCopy;
    offset += bytesToCopy;

    if (offset >= Buffer.byteLength(chunks[i])) {
      i++;
      offset = 0;
    }
  }

  return resultBuffer;
}
```
This function works by iterating over the chunks and copying slices of each one, based on the starting index and remaining byte count. If a chunk becomes null or undefined during execution (i.e., due to removal of data from the buffer list), it will be ignored. This allows for streaming data where data can be removed after the function has been initially called but before completion.

Please note that this implementation does not support synchronous modifications of chunks' contents while the copying is in progress. You may need additional concurrency controls or locking to handle such cases. Also, you might want to optimize memory usage by using `Buffer.allocUnsafe` instead of `Buffer.from` if your application doesn't require data to be securely erased from memory after use.
"]

