["Here is an example of a basic implementation using JavaScript Promises:

```javascript
function loadScript(src) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.async = true;
    script.src = src;

    script.onload = resolve;
    script.onerror = () => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${src}`));

    document.head.appendChild(script);
  });
}

function loadScriptsSequentially(scripts) {
  return scripts.reduce((promise, src) => 
    promise.then(() => loadScript(src)), Promise.resolve());
}

const files = ['file1.js', 'file2.js', 'file3.js']; // replace with your actual filenames
loadScriptsSequentially(files).catch((error) => {
  console.log('Failed to load scripts: ', error);
});
```
This code creates a script element and sets its source and asynchronous attributes, then appends it to the document head. It returns a Promise that resolves when the script is loaded or rejects if there's an error. The `loadScriptsSequentially` function takes an array of scripts and loads them sequentially using reduce. 

This implementation has its trade-offs. Loading files sequentially can be slower than parallel loading because each file must wait for the previous one to finish downloading before it starts downloading the next one. This can lead to a noticeable delay in overall page load time when dealing with many small scripts, but it can also significantly reduce memory usage as the browser only needs to store one script at a time in memory.

The `loadScriptsParallel` function is an example of loading files in parallel for better performance and reduced memory usage, but be aware that it may consume more memory when dealing with many scripts:
```javascript
function loadScriptsParallel(scripts) {
  return Promise.all(scripts.map(src => loadScript(src)));
}
```
This function uses `Promise.all` to wait for all scripts to finish loading before resolving the main promise. This can significantly speed up page load time when dealing with many small scripts, but it will consume more memory since the browser must store all scripts in memory simultaneously.
"]

['Here is an example solution in Java:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.time.*;
import java.io.*;

public class InteractionsProcessor {
    private static final String DELIMITER = ",";
    private Map<String, List<Pair>> userMap = new HashMap<>();

    public void processInteractions(List<String> interactions) throws IOException {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        for (String interaction : interactions) {
            String[] split = interaction.split(DELIMITER);

            if (split.length != 3) {
                continue; // skip invalid entries
            }

            String userId = split[0].trim();
            String actionType = split[1].trim();
            Instant timestamp = Instant.parse(split[2].trim());

            userMap.computeIfAbsent(userId, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(new Pair(actionType, timestamp));
        }

        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Log the time taken for processing each user's interactions
        FileWriter fileWriter = new FileWriter("processing_times.log", true);
        PrintWriter printWriter = new PrintWriter(fileWriter);
        printWriter.println("Processing times: " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
        printWriter.close();
    }

    public Map<String, List<Pair>> getAverageTimesPerAction() {
        Map<String, List<Pair>> averageTimes = new HashMap<>();

        for (Map.Entry<String, List<Pair>> entry : userMap.entrySet()) {
            String userId = entry.getKey();
            List<Pair> interactions = entry.getValue();

            Map<String, Duration> actionTimes = new HashMap<>();
            Pair previousInteraction = null;

            for (Pair interaction : interactions) {
                if (previousInteraction != null && interaction.timestamp.isAfter(previousInteraction.timestamp)) {
                    Duration duration = Duration.between(previousInteraction.timestamp, interaction.timestamp);

                    actionTimes.computeIfAbsent(previousInteraction.actionType, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(duration);
                }

                previousInteraction = interaction;
            }

            List<Pair> averageTimeList = new ArrayList<>();

            for (Map.Entry<String, List<Duration>> actionEntry : actionTimes.entrySet()) {
                String actionType = actionEntry.getKey();
                List<Duration> durations = actionEntry.getValue();

                Duration totalDuration = durations.stream().reduce(Duration::plus).orElseGet(() -> Duration.ZERO);
                Duration averageDuration = totalDuration.dividedBy(durations.size());

                averageTimeList.add(new Pair(actionType, averageDuration));
            }

            averageTimes.put(userId, averageTimeList);
        }

        return averageTimes;
    }

    private static class Pair {
        String actionType;
        Instant timestamp;
        Duration duration;

        Pair(String actionType, Instant timestamp) {
            this.actionType = actionType;
            this.timestamp = timestamp;
        }

        Pair(String actionType, Duration duration) {
            this.actionType = actionType;
            this.duration = duration;
        }
    }
}
```
This Java class processes a list of user interactions and calculates the average time spent per unique action type within each user's session. It uses a `Map` to group the interactions by user ID, and then calculates the average times for each action type in each session. It also includes logging to a file that records the processing times.
']

