['Here is an implementation of your requirements in Java. Please note that for demonstration purposes, this code only logs messages instead of integrating with an actual external logging service like Log4j or Slf4j. The `executeCommand()` method handles command-line arguments, environment variables, and retries. 

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class Main {
    private static final int MAX_RETRIES = 3;
    private static final String[] COMMANDS = {"command1", "command2"};

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        for (String command : COMMANDS) {
            executeCommand(Arrays.asList("/bin/sh", "-c", command), null, new File("."));
        }
    }

    private static boolean executeCommand(List<String> command, String[] envp, File dir) throws Exception {
        int retryCount = 0;
        while (retryCount < MAX_RETRIES) {
            try {
                Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command.toArray(new String[0]), envp, dir);

                BufferedReader stdInput = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
                BufferedReader stdError = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getErrorStream()));

                String s;
                while ((s = stdInput.readLine()) != null) {
                    System.out.println("Output: " + s);
                }
                while ((s = stdError.readLine()) != null) {
                    System.err.println("Error: " + s);
                }

                boolean isCompleted = process.waitFor(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
                if (!isCompleted) {
                    process.destroy();
                    throw new Exception("Command timed out");
                }

                int exitValue = process.exitValue();
                System.out.println("Exit value: " + exitValue);

                // Monitor resource usage
                com.sun.management.OperatingSystemMXBean os = (com.sun.management.OperatingSystemMXBean) ManagementFactory.getOperatingSystemMXBean();
                System.out.println("CPU load: " + os.getProcessCpuLoad() * 100);

                stdInput.close();
                stdError.close();

                return exitValue == 0;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                retryCount++;
                System.err.println("Command failed, retrying..." + retryCount);
                Thread.sleep(1000); // Wait before the next attempt
            }
        }

        throw new Exception("Max retries exceeded");
    }
}
```
This code is a simple demonstration and doesn't handle file deletion or more complex log integration with an external logging service. For a real-world application, you might want to use a full-featured logging framework such as Log4j or SLF4J for better control over log output, filtering, and configuration options.
']

