['Here's an example of how you might implement this in Rust. This code uses a static struct `Configuration` to hold information about the configuration directory, its subdirectories, and environment variables related to these paths. The function `setup_configuration` checks if the main directory exists and is writable. If not, it attempts to create it. Then it verifies the existence of each necessary subdirectory within the main directory and initializes them with default settings, again creating them if they don't exist. 

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::env;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::collections::HashMap;

pub struct Configuration {
    path: String,
    subdirectories: Vec<String>,
}

impl Configuration {
    fn check_and_create(&self) -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
        if !PathBuf::from(env::var("HOME").unwrap()).join(&self.path).exists() {
            fs::create_dir(PathBuf::from(env::var("HOME").unwrap()).join(&self.path))?;
        }
        for subdirectory in &self.subdirectories {
            if !PathBuf::from(env::var("HOME").unwrap()).join(&self.path).join(subdirectory).exists() {
                fs::create_dir(PathBuf::from(env::var("HOME").unwrap()).join(&self.path).join(subdirectory))?;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn setup_configuration(config: Configuration) -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
    config.check_and_create()
}
``` 

This is a simplified example and doesn't handle all edge cases or performance considerations. For instance, in a production environment, you might want to ensure atomicity of operations, use more robust error handling strategies, and consider using an external library for configuration management if one is available for your specific needs.
