['Here is a C code snippet that implements the described functionality. This solution uses dynamic arrays (malloc) to store inputs which allows it to handle large datasets without needing to know the size beforehand. The helper function find_maximum() is used to find and print the maximum value in the array, and calculate_average() calculates and prints the average of all integers. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void find_maximum(int *array, int size) {
    if (size == 0) {
        printf("The input list is empty.\n");
        return;
    }

    int max = array[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < size; i++) {
        if (array[i] > max) {
            max = array[i];
        }
    }

    printf("The largest integer is %d\n", max);
}

void calculate_average(int *array, int size) {
    if (size == 0) {
        return;
    }

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        sum += array[i];
    }

    printf("The average of all integers is %.2f\n", sum / size);
}

void process_input() {
    int capacity = 1, size = 0;
    int *array = (int*) malloc(sizeof(int) * capacity);

    if (array == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        return;
    }

    printf("Enter integers. Enter a non-numeric character to end input:\n");
    while (scanf("%d", &array[size]) == 1) {
        size++;

        if (size == capacity) {
            int *temp = realloc(array, sizeof(int) * 2 * capacity);

            if (temp == NULL) {
                printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
                free(array);
                return;
            }

            array = temp;
            capacity *= 2;
        }
    }

    find_maximum(array, size);
    calculate_average(array, size);
    free(array);
}
```
This function should be used as part of a larger application. To ensure that it works efficiently with external libraries for data manipulation, you may need to adapt this code according to the specific requirements and constraints of your project. Also, please remember that using dynamic allocation can potentially lead to memory leaks if not properly managed which is handled in this function.
