['Here is a basic implementation of `dynamic_log_message`. This code does not cover all features from your list but gives an idea about how it could be implemented in C. A more detailed version would require additional considerations like proper error handling, log rotation, multi-threaded message writing etc.

```c
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <limits.h>
#define MAX_LOG_LEN 1024

// A simple singly linked list implementation for managing log entries
typedef struct LogEntry {
    char* message;
    struct LogEntry* next;
} LogEntry;

LogEntry* head = NULL;
int totalSize = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock; // Mutex for thread-safe logging

// Function to add log entry safely with mutex and size limit check
void dynamic_log_message(const char* format, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, format);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock the mutex for thread-safe logging

    if (totalSize < INT_MAX) { // Check total size to avoid overflowing memory
        char* buffer = malloc(MAX_LOG_LEN);

        int len = vsnprintf(buffer, MAX_LOG_LEN, format, args);
        if (len >= 0 && len < MAX_LOG_LEN) {
            LogEntry* newLog = malloc(sizeof(LogEntry));

            // Add the log entry to the linked list
            newLog->message = buffer;
            newLog->next = head;
            head = newLog;

            totalSize += len + 1; // Update total size of logs
        } else {
            free(buffer);
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock the mutex after logging
    va_end(args);
}
```
Note: This is a basic and simplified implementation for teaching purposes only. In real-world applications, this code would need to be expanded and modified to address all of your requirements. For example, you would likely want to replace `malloc()` with an allocator that provides thread safety, error handling, and memory efficiency. You might also want to introduce a rate limiting mechanism to prevent log flooding from excessive logging activity.
']

