['Here's a basic example of how you might implement such a system using Java. This is a simplified version of what you asked for, but it gives an idea of some core concepts to consider when creating a distributed caching system. Note that this does not include integration with NoSQL databases or error handling, which would be quite complex and beyond the scope of this example:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DistributedCache {
    private static final ScheduledExecutorService scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);
    private static final int INVALIDATION_INTERVAL = 60; // seconds

    private ConcurrentMap<String, CacheEntry> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public DistributedCache() {
        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> invalidateExpiredEntries(), 1, INVALIDATION_INTERVAL, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    public void add(String key, Object value) {
        CacheEntry entry = new CacheEntry(value);
        cache.put(key, entry);
    }

    public void remove(String key) {
        cache.remove(key);
    }

    public Optional<Object> get(String key) {
        CacheEntry entry = cache.get(key);

        if (entry == null) {
            System.out.println("Cache miss: " + key);
            return Optional.empty();
        }

        if (System.currentTimeMillis() > entry.expirationTime) {
            System.out.println("Invalidated cache hit: " + key);

            // fallback to data source
            Object value = getValueFromDataSource(key);
            add(key, value);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Cache hit: " + key);
        }

        return Optional.ofNullable(entry).map(CacheEntry::getValue);
    }

    private Object getValueFromDataSource(String key) {
        // Implement this method to fetch the value from your data source
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Not implemented");
    }

    private void invalidateExpiredEntries() {
        long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        for (Map.Entry<String, CacheEntry> entry : cache.entrySet()) {
            if (currentTime > entry.getValue().expirationTime) {
                remove(entry.getKey());
                System.out.println("Invalidated: " + entry.getKey());
            }
        }
    }

    private static class CacheEntry {
        private Object value;
        private long expirationTime = System.currentTimeMillis() + 60 * 1000; // expires after one minute

        public CacheEntry(Object value) {
            this.value = value;
        }

        public Object getValue() {
            return value;
        }
    }
}
```
Please note that you would need to implement the `getValueFromDataSource` method to fetch data from your background source, and also handle exceptions where necessary (such as when fetching data or adding/removing entries). You might also want to consider using a mature caching library like Ehcache or Guava's CacheBuilder for these features instead of implementing it manually.
