["Here's how you might implement this in C. Due to the complexity of some parts (e.g., thread-safe memory allocation, pseudo-random number generator selection), we will focus on the main logic. Please note that this is a simplified version and does not cover all edge cases or details:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct { /* ... */ } RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
typedef uint32_t RNTI;
typedef uint16_t CRCFLAG;

typedef struct {
    RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG ue_info;
    RNTI rnti;
    struct {
        CRCFLAG crcflag;
        /* ... */
    } crc_tag;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

// Pseudo-random number generator function type
typedef CRCFLAG (*PrngFunc)(RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG ue_info, RNTI rnti);

pthread_mutex_t allocMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* createPDUs(size_t max_pdus, PrngFunc prng, int validate) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&allocMutex);

    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* pdus = NULL;
    size_t allocated = 0;

    for (size_t attempt = 0; allocated < max_pdus && attempt < 10; ++attempt) {
        // Allocate memory for one more PDU
        NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* newPdus = realloc(pdus, (allocated + 1) * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
        if (!newPdus) {
            // Allocation failed, sleep for a while and try again
            struct timespec ts = {.tv_sec = 0, .tv_nsec = 100 * 1000 * 1000}; // 100 ms
            nanosleep(&ts, NULL);
            continue;
        }

        pdus = newPdus;

        // Initialize the PDU
        NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* pdu = &pdus[allocated];
        /* ... initialize ue_info, rnti ... */
        pdu->crc_tag.crcflag = prng(pdu->ue_info, pdu->rnti);

        // Optionally validate the PDU
        if (validate && !validatePDU(*pdu)) {
            continue;
        }

        allocated++;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&allocMutex);

    return pdus;
}
```

In this code, `createPDUs` is a function that dynamically allocates memory for an array of NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structures. It accepts the maximum number of PDUs to allocate (`max_pdus`), a pseudo-random number generator function (`prng`) and whether to validate each PDU before it's returned (`validate`). The locking mechanism ensures that the allocation process is thread-safe.

Apart from ensuring the maximum possible number of unique PDUs can be allocated without exceeding the size constraint, this code also handles memory allocation failures by sleeping and retrying. It checks whether validation is required before each allocation attempt (`validatePDU(*pdu)`). The pseudo-random number generator function is passed as a parameter to allow different implementations at runtime.
