['This is a complex programming task that requires careful design, especially for thread safety and efficient resource management. I'll provide a simplified version using pthreads library for threading, dynamic arrays (vector in C++) and rand() for random number generation. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

typedef struct Record {
    int value;
    time_t timestamp;
} Record;

// Global variables for thread safety and simplicity of code
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int counter = 0;
FILE *logFile = NULL;
Record* records = NULL;

void initRecords(int seed, int num) {
    srand(seed);
    logFile = fopen("log.txt", "w");
    if (logFile == NULL) exit(-1); // handle error
    records = malloc(num * sizeof(Record));
}

void addRecord() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Record rec;
    rec.value = rand();  // replace with actual random value in your use case
    rec.timestamp = time(NULL);

    records[counter++] = rec;

    if (counter % 100 == 0) {
        for (int i = 0; i < counter; ++i) {
            fprintf(logFile, "Record %d: timestamp=%ld, value=%d\n", i, records[i].timestamp, records[i].value);
        }
        fflush(logFile);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

This code maintains a global mutex for thread safety. The `initRecords` function initializes the dynamic array of Record structures, and opens the log file in write mode. 

The `addRecord` function creates a new record with a random value and the current time as timestamp, then adds it to the end of the records list. If there have been 100 operations since the last logging operation, it writes all record data into the log file.

This code doesn't handle memory allocation failures or other potential errors. In production code, you would add appropriate error handling and cleanup mechanisms, possibly using C++ exceptions for more complex scenarios where resources must be cleaned up even in case of an exception. 

