['Creating such a comprehensive code in C that covers all the points mentioned is beyond the scope of this platform due to its complexity and length. However, I will provide a simplified example that you could use as a starting point for your own implementation. 

Note: This example doesn't include all the requested features but provides a basic logging framework using dynamic loading and environment-based configuration. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <unistd.h>

typedef void (*logger_func)(const char *msg);
void* logger_handle;
logger_func log_info, log_warning, log_error;
char backend_path[256];

void init_logging() {
    sprintf(backend_path, "./lib%s.so", getenv("LOGGER_BACKEND") ?: "default");

    logger_handle = dlopen(backend_path, RTLD_NOW);
    if (!logger_handle) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to load logging backend: %s\n", dlerror());
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    log_info = (logger_func)dlsym(logger_handle, "log_info");
    if (!log_info) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to find symbol 'log_info'\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    log_warning = (logger_func)dlsym(logger_handle, "log_warning");
    if (!log_warning) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to find symbol 'log_warning'\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    log_error = (logger_func)dlsym(logger_handle, "log_error");
    if (!log_error) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to find symbol 'log_error'\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}

int main() {
    init_logging();

    log_info("Application started");
    //... other application logic ...

    dlclose(logger_handle);
    return 0;
}
```
This is a simple logging framework where the logger backend can be chosen using an environment variable. It demonstrates dynamic loading of the logging backend and resolving symbols at runtime. A full solution should include more features like error handling, resource constraints, flexible configuration, integration with monitoring frameworks, etc. 

