['Here's a basic outline of how you could structure your C code for this task. Due to its complexity, it cannot be fully represented here and would require a large number of files and modules. You should also consider splitting tasks into separate functions/files based on their responsibilities and use proper data structures for efficient handling. 

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define a structure to store request information
typedef struct {
    char method[10]; // Can be enum if predefined methods are known
    char url[256];
    void* headers;  // Replace with appropriate header data type
    void* body;     // Replace with appropriate body data type
} Request;

// Define a structure to store request handler information
typedef struct {
    int id;
    void (*callback)(void *, Request);
    void* context;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;  // Used for concurrency control
} Handler;

Handler* handlers = NULL;  // Array to store request handlers
int num_handlers = 0;      // Number of current handlers
pthread_mutex_t handler_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;  // Mutex for concurrent access control

void initHandler(int id, void (*callback)(void *, Request), void* context) {
    Handler* new_handler = (Handler*) malloc(sizeof(Handler));
    if(new_handler == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for handler.\n");
        return;
    }
    // Initialize the handler
    pthread_mutex_init(&new_handler->lock, NULL);
    new_handler->id = id;
    new_handler->callback = callback;
    new_handler->context = context;

    // Add the new handler to our array and increment the count
    pthread_mutex_lock(&handler_lock);
    handlers = (Handler*) realloc(handlers, sizeof(Handler) * (num_handlers + 1));
    if(handlers == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for handlers array.\n");
        return;
    }
    handlers[num_handlers++] = new_handler;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&handler_lock);
}
``` 

In this code, we first define the structures to hold our request and handler data. Then we create an `initHandler` function to allocate memory for a new handler, initialize it with the provided callback function pointer and context pointer, and add it to our array of handlers. We use a mutex to ensure that only one thread can access our handler list at a time to prevent race conditions.
']

